## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to handle equipment with strong reflective/transparent surfaces, a realistic industrial setting, and on-device adaptation under tight latency budgets.

## Key Concepts
- Reflective/transparent object pose estimation
- Differentiable rendering for photometric consistency
- On-device 3-shot adaptation via compact adapters
- Synthetic+real data with material randomization
- Edge latency and robustness under glare

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch
function forward(frame) {
  const feats = backbone(frame);
  const seg = segHead(feats);
  const pose = poseHead(feats);
  const render = differentiableRenderer(pose, frame);
  return {seg, pose, loss: computeLoss(render, frame)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to varying refractive indices?
- How would you quantify uncertainty in pose under specular highlights?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine learned features with classical cues under tight on-device latency, in a realistic retail robotics setting.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight CNN + classical feature fusion
- On-device few-shot adaptation (3-5 shots)
- Latency budgeting (<=50 ms/frame)
- Robust evaluation: per-item accuracy, misorientation metrics, throughput

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch of forward pass
function forward(frame) {
  const rois = detectROIs(frame);
  for (const roi of rois) {
    const cnnFeat = smallCNN(roi);
    const cues = extractCues(roi); // hist, edges, texture
    const fused = fuse(cnnFeat, cues);
    const label = orientationHead(fused);
    if (label === 'misoriented') alert(roi);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle label drift with new item designs?
- What ablation studies would you run to verify cue contributions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end CV design with 3D reconstruction, uncertainty, and fast on-device adaptation, crucial for tool manipulation in cluttered spaces.

## Key Concepts
- 3D pose + mesh prediction from monocular input
- Uncertainty estimation and active adaptation
- Edge-efficient architectures and losses for consistency

## Code Example
```javascript
function loss(params){
  const L2 = reprojLoss(params);
  const Lpose = poseLoss(params);
  const Lmesh = meshLoss(params);
  const Lreg = temporalEdgeReg(params);
  return L2 + Lpose + Lmesh + Lreg;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you calibrate uncertainty for active-learning? 
- What would you change for multiple simultaneous tools?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical on-device CV with privacy constraints, latency pressure, and data privacy.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight models, ROI extraction, temporal reasoning
- Quantization, pruning, on-device inference
- Federated learning, differential privacy, privacy budgets
- Evaluation: latency, memory, F1/precision-recall under occlusion

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: frame window prep for on-device gesture model
function prepareWindow(frames) {
  const ROI = detectHandROI(frames[0]);
  const window = frames.slice(-16).map(f => crop(f, ROI));
  return stack(window);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure and mitigate drift when lighting changes across devices?
- How would you handle new gestures without retraining all devices?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a compact computer vision pipeline with practical on-device adaptation under tight latency constraints. It also probes understanding of data strategy and evaluation beyond single-object pose tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight detection with 2D bounding boxes
- Depth proxy estimation from object size
- Simple tracking with temporal consistency
- On-device few-shot adaptation (3-shot)
- Counting metrics and evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: detection + tracking update loop
for (const frame of video) {
  const detections = detector.predict(frame);
  const depthProxy = estimateDepth(detections, calibration);
  const tracked = tracker.update(detections, depthProxy);
  yield { boxes: tracked, count: countPackages(tracked) };
}
```

